{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_choosing-the-state-structure","statement":"One principle for structuring state is to group related state, merging two state variables into one if they always change together.","source_span":"**Group related state.** If you always update two or more state variables at the same time, consider merging them into a single state variable."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_choosing-the-state-structure","statement":"A second principle is to avoid contradictions in state, since state that can disagree with itself leaves room for mistakes.","source_span":"**Avoid contradictions in state.** When the state is structured in a way that several pieces of state may contradict and \"disagree\" with each other, you leave room for mistakes."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_choosing-the-state-structure","statement":"A third principle is to avoid redundant state — information calculable from props or existing state during rendering shouldn't be stored in state.","source_span":"**Avoid redundant state.** If you can calculate some information from the component's props or its existing state variables during rendering, you should not put that information into that component's state."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_choosing-the-state-structure","statement":"A fourth principle is to avoid duplication in state, since duplicated data across state variables is hard to keep in sync.","source_span":"**Avoid duplication in state.** When the same data is duplicated between multiple state variables, or within nested objects, it is difficult to keep them in sync."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_choosing-the-state-structure","statement":"A fifth principle is to avoid deeply nested state, preferring a flat structure since deeply hierarchical state is inconvenient to update.","source_span":"**Avoid deeply nested state.** Deeply hierarchical state is not very convenient to update. When possible, prefer to structure state in a flat way."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_choosing-the-state-structure","statement":"In the feedback form example, the isSending and isSent boolean state variables are replaced with a single status variable that can be 'typing', 'sending', or 'sent'.","source_span":"it is better to replace them with one `status` state variable that may take one of *three* valid states:** `'typing'` (initial), `'sending'`, and `'sent'`"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_choosing-the-state-structure","statement":"The reason for replacing isSending/isSent with status is that isSending and isSent should never be true at the same time.","source_span":"Since `isSending` and `isSent` should never be `true` at the same time, it is better to replace them with one `status` state variable"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_choosing-the-state-structure","statement":"If a state variable is an object, you cannot update only one field in it without explicitly copying the other fields; e.g., calling setPosition({x: 100}) would lose the y property entirely.","source_span":"you can't do `setPosition({ x: 100 })` in the above example because it would not have the `y` property at all!"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_choosing-the-state-structure","statement":"To update only x in an object state, you should either do setPosition({...position, x: 100}) or split into two state variables and call setX(100).","source_span":"you would either do `setPosition({ ...position, x: 100 })`, or split them into two state variables and do `setX(100)`"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_choosing-the-state-structure","statement":"The document paraphrases Albert Einstein to summarize the goal of state structuring: \"Make your state as simple as it can be--but no simpler.\"","source_span":"To paraphrase Albert Einstein, **\"Make your state as simple as it can be--but no simpler.\"**"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_choosing-the-state-structure","fact_refs":[0,1,2,3,4],"question":"What are the five principles for structuring state well according to this guide?","answer":"Group related state, avoid contradictions in state, avoid redundant state, avoid duplication in state, and avoid deeply nested state."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_choosing-the-state-structure","fact_refs":[5],"question":"In the feedback form example, what three values can the single 'status' state variable take?","answer":"'typing' (the initial state), 'sending', and 'sent'."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_choosing-the-state-structure","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Why does the guide recommend replacing isSending and isSent with one status variable?","answer":"Because isSending and isSent should never both be true at the same time, so combining them into one status variable avoids contradictory states."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_choosing-the-state-structure","fact_refs":[7],"question":"Why can't you call setPosition({x: 100}) to update only the x field of a position state object?","answer":"Because it would replace the whole state object and it would not have the y property at all."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_choosing-the-state-structure","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What are the two ways suggested to update only the x property of a position state object?","answer":"Either call setPosition({...position, x: 100}) to copy the other fields, or split the state into two separate variables and call setX(100)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_choosing-the-state-structure","fact_refs":[9],"question":"What paraphrased quote does the document use to summarize the goal of good state structure?","answer":"\"Make your state as simple as it can be--but no simpler,\" paraphrasing Albert Einstein."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_conditional-rendering","statement":"React lets you conditionally render JSX using JavaScript syntax like if statements, &&, and ?: (ternary) operators.","source_span":"In React, you can conditionally render JSX using JavaScript syntax like `if` statements, `&&`, and `? :` operators."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_conditional-rendering","statement":"A component can return null to conditionally render nothing at all.","source_span":"In this case, you can return `null`"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_conditional-rendering","statement":"In the PackingList example, a checkmark (✅) is appended to an item's name when its isPacked prop is true.","source_span":"You want to add a checkmark (✅) to packed items if `isPacked={true}`."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_conditional-rendering","statement":"The ternary operator can render a checkmark conditionally with the expression {isPacked ? name + ' ✅' : name}.","source_span":"return (\n  <li className=\"item\">\n    {isPacked ? name + ' ✅' : name}\n  </li>\n);"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_conditional-rendering","statement":"JSX elements aren't considered \"instances\" because they don't hold any internal state and aren't real DOM nodes — they are lightweight descriptions, like blueprints.","source_span":"JSX elements aren't \"instances\" because they don't hold any internal state and aren't real DOM nodes. They're lightweight descriptions, like blueprints."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_conditional-rendering","statement":"The ?: operator is also known as the \"conditional operator\" or \"ternary operator\".","source_span":"the [conditional operator](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Conditional_Operator) or \"ternary operator\""}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_conditional-rendering","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What three JavaScript syntax constructs does React use to conditionally render JSX?","answer":"if statements, the && operator, and the ?: (ternary) operator."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_conditional-rendering","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What can a React component return if it wants to render nothing at all?","answer":"It can return null."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_conditional-rendering","fact_refs":[2],"question":"In the PackingList example, what symbol is added to an item when its isPacked prop is true?","answer":"A checkmark (✅) is added to the item's name."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_conditional-rendering","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Why does the document say JSX elements aren't 'instances' even when the same conditional JSX appears in two branches?","answer":"Because JSX elements don't hold any internal state and aren't real DOM nodes — they're lightweight descriptions, like blueprints, so equivalent JSX in different branches is completely equivalent."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_conditional-rendering","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What ternary expression renders a checkmark next to an item's name only when isPacked is true?","answer":"{isPacked ? name + ' ✅' : name}"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_conditional-rendering","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What is another name for the ?: operator mentioned in the conditional rendering guide?","answer":"It is also called the \"conditional operator\" or \"ternary operator\"."}
